The pear cut vs emerald cut diamond comparison is one of the most misunderstood in the market.

Both shapes elongate the finger. Both come in elongated proportions. Both look striking in platinum solitaires.

They are completely different diamonds.

The pear cut is a brilliant cut — a hybrid of the round brilliant and the marquise. Its 58 facets scatter light in every direction, creating active sparkle and high fire. The emerald cut is a step cut with long, parallel facets that create a hall-of-mirrors reflection.

One is lively. One is calm.

The pear also carries a risk that no step cut ever has: the bow tie. A dark shadow across the center of the stone that ranges from barely visible to deeply distracting.

Evaluating bow tie in a pear requires watching video. There is no way to assess it from a certificate alone.

This guide covers both shapes completely — geometry, bow tie risk, the pointed tip and why it matters, certified market pricing from 1ct to 4ct, clarity floors, color minimums, and exactly which buyer should choose which shape.

---

**TL;DR**

- Pear: brilliant cut, 58 facets, scattered sparkle, bow tie risk, pointed tip requiring prong protection, VS2 clarity floor.
- Emerald: step cut, hall-of-mirrors, no bow tie, VS1 clarity minimum, G color minimum in platinum.
- Natural pear 1ct GIA D-VS1 from [$3,803](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29112328?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Natural emerald 1ct GIA D-VS1 from [$3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).
- Lab pear 1ct IGI D-VVS1 from [$1,006](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26932733?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Lab pear 2ct IGI D-VVS1 from [$2,692](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27379422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).
- → Choose pear if you want maximum sparkle, a unique silhouette, and don't mind evaluating bow tie. → Choose emerald if you want the quiet, elegant hall-of-mirrors look and accept the clarity premium.

---

## Expert Summary

The pear cut is one of the oldest fancy shapes. It was developed in the 15th century and remains among the most recognizable silhouettes in fine jewelry today. Its teardrop outline — round on one end, pointed on the other — creates an elongating effect on the finger that buyers specifically seek out.

Its 58 facets make it optically active.

Color and inclusions are scattered and broken up by the brilliant facets, giving the pear more forgiveness on clarity and color than any step cut.

The emerald cut is an Art Deco icon. Its long rectangular body with cropped corners and step-cut facets creates a window into the stone. Light enters, bounces between the parallel step facets, and exits in slow, dramatic planes of reflection.

It is one of the most elegant diamonds you can buy.

It is also one of the most demanding. Inclusions are visible. Color reads as warmth.

The emerald cut magnifies both flaws and beauty equally.

At natural 1ct, certified market inventory shows pear starting at [$3,803](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29112328?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) GIA D-VS1 and emerald at [$3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) GIA D-VS1. Emerald is cheaper at top grades because buyers don't pay a shape premium the way they do for popular fancy shapes like pear and oval.

At practical buying grades — where you match quality to what the cut can actually hide — pear allows lower clarity and lower color. Buying a pear at G-VS2 is sensible. Buying an emerald at G-VS2 is a visual compromise.

---

## Shape Geometry: How Pear and Emerald Cut Are Built

The pear cut, also called the teardrop cut, combines the pavilion of a round brilliant with the elongated outline of the marquise. It has one pointed culet at the tip and a rounded base. The standard pear has 58 facets arranged to maximize light return and fire.

It is a fancy shape brilliant cut.

That classification means inclusions are scattered by the facets, color is broken into sparkle, and light performance is high. The trade-off is bow tie risk — a dark optical shadow across the center that every pear buyer must evaluate before committing.

The emerald cut is a step cut. Its body is rectangular with cropped corners — octagonal in outline — and its facets are arranged in parallel horizontal rows on the crown and pavilion. There are 57–58 facets, but their geometry is completely different from a brilliant cut.

Light does not scatter in an emerald cut.

It reflects in long, slow planes. The result is the hall-of-mirrors effect — a deep, mirror-like reflection that makes the stone look like it has internal depth.

