## The Bow Tie Problem

Every pear cut diamond has a bow tie.

The only question is how severe it is.

A bow tie is a dark optical shadow that forms across the center of pear, oval, and marquise diamonds. It appears because light entering the elongated middle section of the stone escapes from the pavilion at an angle that the eye reads as darkness. The shape of the shadow looks like a bow tie — two dark triangles meeting at the center.

A light, barely visible bow tie is acceptable and normal. A severe bow tie — dark, prominent, visible from across a room — kills the beauty of the stone.

The severity of the bow tie is determined by the specific proportions of each individual stone. It cannot be predicted from a GIA certificate. Certificate data does not capture bow tie severity.

You must watch a video of the specific stone before buying any pear cut diamond.

> **Farzana says:** "I've seen buyers choose a pear diamond online from photos and be shocked when they receive it. Photos hide bow tie. Video shows it. If the vendor doesn't have a video of the specific stone, don't buy it — buy from someone who does."

The emerald cut has zero bow tie risk. Its step facets do not create the optical geometry that produces the shadow. Buyers who specifically want to avoid bow tie evaluation should choose the emerald cut, or choose a brilliant cut with symmetrical shape (like round or cushion).

**Bow tie severity guide:**

<table class="dc-custom-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Severity</th>
<th>Appearance</th>
<th>Acceptable?</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>None / Barely visible</td><td>Invisible or faint shadow in direct overhead light only</td><td>Yes — ideal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Light</td><td>Visible as a soft shadow in some lighting angles</td><td>Yes — acceptable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moderate</td><td>Clearly visible, detracts from brilliance</td><td>Borderline — evaluate carefully</td></tr>
<tr><td>Severe</td><td>Dark, prominent, visible from any angle</td><td>No — reject this stone</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Bow tie in pear diamonds is not mentioned on GIA or IGI certificates. The only way to assess it is to watch video of the specific stone rotating under mixed lighting.

