## The Point Factor

The pear cut's most distinctive feature is its pointed tip.

That tip is both the shape's greatest asset and its greatest vulnerability.

On the asset side, the pointed tip creates a stronger finger-elongating effect than any other shape of equivalent carat weight. When worn tip-toward-the-nail (the traditional orientation), the point visually extends the finger dramatically. This is why pear cuts are popular with buyers who prioritize the look of a long, elegant hand.

The tip can also be worn tip-toward-the-wrist in the east-west orientation — a modern setting style that changes the silhouette entirely and protects the tip by pointing it away from daily impact exposure.

On the vulnerability side, the pointed tip of a pear diamond is a chip risk. The crystalline structure of diamond does not protect a fine point the way it protects a rounded or cropped corner. An exposed pear tip — one covered only by a single prong or no prong at all — is vulnerable to chipping from impact.

The solution is a V-prong at the tip.

> **Farzana says:** "Every pear cut ring you buy needs a V-prong at the pointed tip. It's not optional. A claw or double-claw prong at the tip protects the most vulnerable part of the stone. I always check the tip prong before I recommend a setting."

The emerald cut has cropped corners rather than pointed tips. This makes it significantly more durable at the corners than a pear, though the corners do still require prong coverage.

A poorly set emerald cut corner that is exposed will chip from lateral impact. The difference is that a cropped corner is far more resistant than a sharp point.

**Tip and corner comparison:**

<table class="dc-custom-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Pear Cut</th>
<th>Emerald Cut</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Vulnerable point</td><td>Pointed tip — high chip risk</td><td>Cropped corners — low chip risk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Required prong</td><td>V-prong at tip (mandatory)</td><td>Prong at each corner (4–6 prongs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Orientation options</td><td>Tip-up (traditional) or tip-down (east-west)</td><td>Standard vertical or east-west</td></tr>
<tr><td>Finger elongation</td><td>Strong — point visually extends finger</td><td>Moderate — length creates illusion</td></tr>
<tr><td>Durability rating</td><td>Lower — tip is most fragile point</td><td>Higher — no sharp points</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

