At 1ct, emerald cut is cheaper than pear at comparable top grades. At 2ct–3ct, pear and emerald converge more closely than buyers expect.

The reason: pear cuts at 2ct+ require significant rough and strong GIA cut evaluation; buyers pay for the shape, but the price premium compresses at larger sizes.

The smarter comparison is at practical grades — what you'd actually buy. A G-VS2 pear at 1ct is eye-clean and well under the D-VS1 prices shown above.

A G-VS1 emerald is the minimum acceptable grade. That practical-grade gap is where pear wins on value.

> **Farzana says:** "Never compare pear and emerald at the same grade on paper. They don't need the same grade to look equally clean. A G-VS2 pear and a G-VS1 emerald look the same to the naked eye — but the pear costs hundreds less. Shop for what the cut needs, not what sounds equivalent on paper."

