## Clarity: What Each Shape Hides

The pear cut's brilliant facets fragment and scatter inclusions. A VS2 inclusion in the center table of a pear will be difficult to see with the naked eye. An SI1 inclusion near the girdle of a pear is often completely invisible face-up.

The emerald cut has no such forgiveness.

Its large open table and step-cut facets act like a magnifying glass. VS1 is the practical minimum for emerald cut. VS2 can work if the inclusion is positioned near the girdle or edge of the table — but always review the clarity plot on the certificate and check video before accepting VS2 in an emerald.

**Clarity floor comparison:**

<table class="dc-custom-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Clarity</th>
<th>Pear Cut</th>
<th>Emerald Cut</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>VS1</td><td>Safe — eye-clean guaranteed</td><td>Safe — recommended floor</td></tr>
<tr><td>VS2</td><td>Safe — typically eye-clean</td><td>Borderline — check clarity plot</td></tr>
<tr><td>SI1</td><td>Often eye-clean — check video</td><td>Not recommended — likely visible</td></tr>
<tr><td>SI2</td><td>Risky — some visible inclusions</td><td>Avoid</td></tr>
<tr><td>I1</td><td>Avoid</td><td>Avoid</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Pear buyers can save significantly by going to VS2 or SI1 when the inclusion location is favorable. Emerald buyers cannot take that shortcut — VS1 is the floor for a reason.

---

## Color: How Each Shape Reads

The pear cut's brilliant facets break body color into scattered light. An H color pear in white gold or platinum is virtually indistinguishable from a G color pear to the naked eye. Buyers can safely go to H — and often to I — without noticeable warmth.

The emerald cut's open step facets show body color clearly.

G is the safe minimum for emerald in white gold or platinum. H reads as faintly warm in platinum, which some buyers find acceptable and others find distracting. In yellow gold, both shapes can tolerate warmer color — H or I for pear, H for emerald — because the metal's warmth neutralizes minor body color.

