## Proportions: What to Target

For pear cuts, the length-to-width ratio is the most important proportion to evaluate. A ratio of 1.45–1.75 is the accepted ideal range — producing an elongated teardrop that looks balanced rather than too stubby (below 1.45) or too thin and needle-like (above 1.75).

Buyers who want a more dramatic elongation choose 1.65–1.75.

Buyers who want a fuller, rounder teardrop choose 1.45–1.55. Neither is wrong — it comes down to personal preference and the shape of the buyer's finger.

For emerald cuts, 1.30–1.50 is the target range, with 1.35–1.45 being the most popular. A 1.50 emerald looks notably elongated and finger-slimming. A 1.30 emerald looks almost square and boxy.

**Proportion targets:**

<table class="dc-custom-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Shape</th>
<th>L:W Ratio</th>
<th>Effect</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
