## Face-Up Size: Which Looks Bigger?

Both pear and emerald cuts face up larger than a round brilliant of the same carat weight. This is because both are elongated shapes that spread the weight across a longer surface area.

The pear has a slightly larger face-up appearance than the emerald at equivalent carat weight. This is because the pear's round base and pointed tip distribute weight across a longer diagonal axis.

A 1ct pear typically measures approximately 8.0×5.5mm face-up. A 1ct emerald typically measures approximately 7.0×5.0mm. The pear appears larger — though the difference is subtle at 1ct and more pronounced at 2ct+.

---

## Settings: What Works Best

Pear cuts have specific setting requirements that don't apply to most other shapes.

The tip requires a V-prong. No exceptions.

Beyond the tip, pear cuts work well in solitaire settings with 5 prongs (4 on the body, 1 V-prong at tip), in halo settings where the halo continues around the curved base and along the sides, and in east-west settings where the stone is rotated 90 degrees and the tip faces sideways. Three-stone settings with tapered baguette or trapezoid side stones complement the pear's silhouette well.

Emerald cuts look best in settings that echo their clean, angular geometry. A four-prong solitaire with clean, straight prongs is the most popular setting for emerald cuts. Bezel settings — where a thin metal band surrounds the entire stone — look modern and protective.

Three-stone settings with baguette side stones are the most historically accurate pairing, referencing the Art Deco aesthetic that the emerald cut represents.

> **Farzana says:** "The emerald cut bezel setting is one of the most underrated looks in fine jewelry. It protects the corners, keeps the stone low, and looks incredibly clean. If you want modern minimalism with maximum elegance, bezel an emerald cut."

---

